  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{3}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{3}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{3}^{2x-1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{-1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{2x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot ({3}^{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14260591/assets/d23d39f648ceb829fbe26a318e816a46fd41a067ace3547cde828de46d57233d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{3}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
